# serial_relay -- a chain: a client, a relay that both asks and answers, a backend.
#
# Every declaration in this arc so far has been one hop wide. serial_duplex
# gave a pair two directions; serial_two_clients gave one server two
# clients. In both, every domain is either an asker or an answerer, and the
# region it writes says which.
#
# This declaration seats the first domain that is BOTH. `relay_virt`
# answers `client` and asks `backend` in the same run, so its two roles have
# to live somewhere that cannot confuse them -- and here they live in
# separate regions, which is the only place isolation is ever real.
#
# Read the grants and the shape falls out without a single role word being
# declared. `ask_up` flows client -> relay; `ask_down` flows relay ->
# backend; `answer_down` flows backend -> relay; `answer_up` flows relay ->
# client. One domain appears on both sides of two different peers, and that
# domain is the relay.
#
# `taken` is the client's own tail -- the one number about answers that the
# client alone may write. The relay may read it and never move it, which is
# the stall ring's law carried one hop further: the side that bears a loss
# is the only side that can report it drained.
#
# The two ends share nothing. `client` and `backend` are joined by no
# channel and hold no grant on a single region in common, so a two-hop chain
# can never quietly collapse into one hop. What that isolation costs is
# named honestly by the ring: a client that shares no memory with the far
# end cannot attest the far end, and takes the author of an answer on the
# relay's word alone.
#
# Read whole by `caravan/read.rye`; exercised by `caravan/relay.rye`.

system serial_relay

domain client
domain relay_virt
domain backend

channel relay_virt client
channel relay_virt backend

region ask_up 4096
region ask_down 4096
region answer_down 4096
region answer_up 4096
region taken 64

grant ask_up client rw
grant ask_up relay_virt r
grant ask_down relay_virt rw
grant ask_down backend r
grant answer_down backend rw
grant answer_down relay_virt r
grant answer_up relay_virt rw
grant answer_up client r
grant taken client rw
grant taken relay_virt r
